package service

// Captured native-CLI TLS ClientHello records, stored as full TLS record hex
// (5-byte record header + ClientHello body). These are reconstructed into a
// utls.ClientHelloSpec at dial time via Fingerprinter.FingerprintClientHello,
// then replayed with utls.HelloCustom + ApplyPreset, so the handshake shape
// (cipher list, extension order, curves, ALPN, TLS versions) matches the real
// client rather than a browser preset.
//
// claudeCodeClientHello: captured 2026-09-27 from Claude Code 2.1.282 running on
// Node.js v24.19.0 (the @anthropic-ai/sdk over node's built-in fetch/undici).
// Structure: 17 cipher suites (TLS 1.3 + 1.2), 13 extensions in order
// SNI, extended_master_secret, renegotiation_info, supported_groups,
// ec_point_formats, session_ticket, ALPN[http/1.1], status_request,
// signature_algorithms, SCT, key_share, psk_key_exchange_modes,
// supported_versions[TLS1.3,TLS1.2]. Parses without AllowBluntMimicry. The SNI
// server name is rewritten per-connection from the utls.Config.ServerName, so
// the captured "localhost" name is irrelevant. This ALPN offers only http/1.1,
// so the fingerprint client speaks HTTP/1.1 upstream, matching Claude Code.
//
// codexCLIClientHello: captured 2026-09-27 from Codex CLI 0.156.1 on Windows 11.
// Codex's HTTP client is reqwest with the default native-tls backend, which on
// Windows is SChannel capped at TLS 1.2 — so this is TLS 1.2 only, 18 cipher
// suites, 7 extensions (SNI, supported_groups, ec_point_formats,
// signature_algorithms, session_ticket, extended_master_secret,
// renegotiation_info) and no ALPN, hence HTTP/1.1 upstream. It was first taken
// for antivirus interception; a re-capture with encrypted-connection scanning
// off was byte-identical, and the binary links native-tls + schannel. Codex on
// Linux/macOS uses that platform's TLS library and handshakes differently.
// Fifteen captures differed only in the client random.
const codexCLIClientHello = ""

const claudeCodeClientHello = ""
